00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:02.640
so first of all I would just like to quickly dive&nbsp;
a bit more detail into the requirements we MPD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:08.820
data scientists have for our day-to-day work and&nbsp;
generally we can separate our task into either the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.560
regulatory side of things or the exploratory side&nbsp;
of things and on the regulatory side of things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:19.560
there's really four main deliverables we need to&nbsp;
create the first one is called sdtm so sctm is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.540
industry standard for the data we collect the&nbsp;
clinical trials so regardless of how the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:29.460
is collected in the end when you submit it to&nbsp;
the health authority it has to be in this data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:34.920
um standard such that it's harmonized across&nbsp;
companies next up we have the analysis or atom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:40.800
data sets and here you take the sctm data sets&nbsp;
as source and then you transform them in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:46.320
that suits your analysis Downstream so as a quick&nbsp;
example imagine you collect a questionnaire which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.480
has 10 questions and an overall summary score so&nbsp;
the 10 questions you would have in your satn data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:56.760
because that's the raw data as collected but then&nbsp;
you would calculate the summary overall score as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:02.760
part of your atom transformation next up we need&nbsp;
to perform a bunch of statistical analyzes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:07.260
that can be something as simple and descriptive&nbsp;
as counting Adverse Events or something much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:11.880
more complex for example fitting a survival&nbsp;
model or fitting a mixed measures repeat model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.000
and finally we need to put those statistical&nbsp;
analysis into an easily digestible format and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.400
that's generally either tables listings or graphs&nbsp;
and all these four components combined make up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:28.560
the packages that we submit to health authorities&nbsp;
such as the US FDA in order to get Market approval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.220
for novel Therapeutics we are working on so&nbsp;
this is really at the core of our business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.160
but of course we also have the exploratory set&nbsp;
of things and here really it's highly context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:42.720
context dependent what we need to perform&nbsp;
and therefore we need flexible tools and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:46.860
really the best tool for the job so having&nbsp;
an open source background where you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:51.420
access to multiple languages for example&nbsp;
is really a good thing in this context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.360
we also need to consider that there's novel data&nbsp;
modalities um we're getting more and more for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:02.760
example Imaging data is becoming rapidly and more&nbsp;
on our trials digital biomarker oh mix data Etc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:08.040
so it's not just the classical rectangular data&nbsp;
anymore which we use to analyze in our commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:15.780
software and finally um we want not only to enable&nbsp;
our data scientists to easily explore our data but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:21.240
also people who don't work in data science our&nbsp;
colleagues in safety or clinical scientists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:27.120
we want to really enable them to easily explore&nbsp;
their data and do so in a reproducible manner such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:31.680
that they don't always have to come back to us as&nbsp;
data science and we have this long feedback loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:37.560
and really all of this combined necessity that we&nbsp;
created a Next Generation statistical Computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:43.920
platform where we could really perform all these&nbsp;
analyzes and this is where ocean comes into play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:49.980
our one Central analytics environment which is the&nbsp;
home for both our exploratory and Regulatory work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:57.180
so ocean is built in the cloud on AWS and it's&nbsp;
a language agnostic platform which right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:03.000
supports our python as well as SAS for our Legacy&nbsp;
work but it is easily extendable because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:07.680
based on Docker containers so if you spin up a&nbsp;
container with for example Julia in it suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:12.780
you would have access to that language as&nbsp;
well for Version Control we are using git&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:17.040
and gitlab and we're not only using that for&nbsp;
our packages we develop internally but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.500
actually will be there to store the code for&nbsp;
all our studies so if on a study you program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:27.240
a particular atom data set or an adverse event&nbsp;
table the code for that will be stored on gitlab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:34.980
next up and this is really a big change even&nbsp;
though it sounds somewhat silly but we moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:41.640
away from this proprietary format set 7B that that&nbsp;
used to be um the only format really that we used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:46.620
to store our serious data and instead we opted&nbsp;
for an open source format called parquet which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:51.900
is developed by the Apache foundation and it's a&nbsp;
binary format for handling rectangular data and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:56.340
it's highly efficient in both reading and writing&nbsp;
and it has Surplus already very well so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.620
we have an internal rstudio connect server&nbsp;
as part of this platform where we house our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:08.340
exploratory shiny apps and we have a validated our&nbsp;
package repository so while typically you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:13.920
install packages from cran internally we use this&nbsp;
validated package repository and you would find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:18.840
lot of packages that you know from cran such&nbsp;
as ggplot2 or D player over there as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:24.180
well as our own open source packages we develop&nbsp;
plus internal packages and it goes through and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:29.940
our own internal really streamlined and efficient&nbsp;
validation process which is called Auto validate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.360
then we are using another open source tool called&nbsp;
snake mate to orchestrate our production runs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:42.480
snake make is very clever in a way because it&nbsp;
creates a directed acyclical graph of all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:47.280
artifacts that you need to create so think&nbsp;
of your sctms your atoms your tlgs and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:52.680
it really paralyzes as much as possible taking&nbsp;
dependencies into account so what used to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:58.740
maybe a whole day for example to run everything&nbsp;
for a final clinical study report now is done in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:05.340
a matter of hours if not minutes and finally&nbsp;
ocean is the tool as the home of our next-gen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:10.800
tools for clinical reporting and I will spend the&nbsp;
rest of my part talking about these next-gen tools

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:19.320
so the first one of those is called Oak and Oak&nbsp;
is our solution for automating sdcm mapping so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:25.380
sctm mapping used to take a considerable amount&nbsp;
of our time and it was a rather labor intensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:30.600
process that was also somewhat mundane so we&nbsp;
really opted to streamline this by creating an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:36.600
automation solution and driven by metadata and&nbsp;
our global data standards at Roche Oak can now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:43.680
automate around 80 of sctm domains with around 22&nbsp;
reusable mapping algorithms and this in turn then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:49.260
really saved us a lot of time we've seen already&nbsp;
efficiency gains by at least 50 percent so this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:54.660
is really huge and finally it's actually a fairly&nbsp;
